1
00:00:00,240 --> 00:00:03,159
Hi,  and  welcome  back  to  act.  When

2
00:00:03,159 --> 00:00:04,680
an  initiative  is  finished,  the

3
00:00:04,680 --> 00:00:06,759
experience  can  still  be  useful  if

4
00:00:06,759 --> 00:00:08,920
it  is  explained  clearly.  You  may

5
00:00:08,920 --> 00:00:10,000
need  to  describe  it  to  a

6
00:00:10,000 --> 00:00:12,200
colleague,  a  community  member,  an

7
00:00:12,200 --> 00:00:14,440
organization,  or  simply  someone  who

8
00:00:14,440 --> 00:00:16,960
wants  to  understand  what  happened.

9
00:00:16,960 --> 00:00:18,840
Good  communication  is  not  about

10
00:00:18,840 --> 00:00:20,920
making  the  project  sound  perfect.

11
00:00:20,920 --> 00:00:22,600
It  is  about  helping  another  person

12
00:00:22,600 --> 00:00:24,320
follow  the  story  from  the  original

13
00:00:24,320 --> 00:00:26,599
problem  to  the  lesson  learned.

14
00:00:26,599 --> 00:00:28,400
Clear  communication  allows  the

15
00:00:28,400 --> 00:00:30,519
experience  to  become  useful.  beyond

16
00:00:30,519 --> 00:00:31,720
the  moment  when  the  activity

17
00:00:31,720 --> 00:00:33,920
itself  ended.  It  can  also  help

18
00:00:33,920 --> 00:00:35,680
others  learn  from  what  worked  and

19
00:00:35,680 --> 00:00:36,666
what  did  not.

20
00:00:36,946 --> 00:00:38,666
A  simple  structure  makes  that

21
00:00:38,666 --> 00:00:41,306
easier.  Start  with  the  problem.

22
00:00:41,306 --> 00:00:42,746
What  was  happening  and  who  was

23
00:00:42,746 --> 00:00:45,305
affected?  Then  explain  the  need

24
00:00:45,305 --> 00:00:46,986
you  identified  and  the  response

25
00:00:46,986 --> 00:00:48,225
you  chose.

26
00:00:48,225 --> 00:00:50,225
After  that,  describe  what  happened

27
00:00:50,225 --> 00:00:52,185
when  the  idea  was  carried  out.

28
00:00:52,185 --> 00:00:54,346
Share  the  important  result,  then

29
00:00:54,346 --> 00:00:56,626
finish  with  the  main  lesson.

30
00:00:56,626 --> 00:00:58,826
This  creates  a  clear  path  instead

31
00:00:58,826 --> 00:01:00,306
of  a  collection  of  disconnected

32
00:01:00,306 --> 00:01:01,506
details.

33
00:01:01,506 --> 00:01:02,986
You  do  not  need  to  include

34
00:01:02,986 --> 00:01:05,226
everything.  Include  the  information

35
00:01:05,226 --> 00:01:06,705
that  helps  another  person

36
00:01:06,705 --> 00:01:08,185
understand  why  the  initiative

37
00:01:08,185 --> 00:01:10,026
mattered,  and  what  the  experience

38
00:01:10,026 --> 00:01:11,225
taught  you.

39
00:01:11,225 --> 00:01:12,946
Clear  structure  helps  the  listeners

40
00:01:12,946 --> 00:01:14,466
stay  with  you  from  start  to

41
00:01:14,466 --> 00:01:15,374
finish.

42
00:01:15,614 --> 00:01:16,854
There  are  different  ways  to

43
00:01:16,854 --> 00:01:19,134
communicate  the  same  experience.

44
00:01:19,134 --> 00:01:20,733
You  might  use  a  short  spoken

45
00:01:20,733 --> 00:01:23,213
explanation,  a  few  slides,  a

46
00:01:23,213 --> 00:01:25,654
sequence  of  photos,  a  drawing,  a

47
00:01:25,654 --> 00:01:28,294
video,  or  a  simple  story.  The

48
00:01:28,294 --> 00:01:30,654
format  is  not  the  goal.

49
00:01:30,654 --> 00:01:31,974
Choose  the  format  that  makes  the

50
00:01:31,974 --> 00:01:34,614
experience  easiest  to  understand.  A

51
00:01:34,614 --> 00:01:36,294
complex  format  does  not

52
00:01:36,294 --> 00:01:37,534
automatically  make  the  message

53
00:01:37,534 --> 00:01:39,934
stronger.  A  simple  format  is  not

54
00:01:39,934 --> 00:01:41,294
weak  if  it  communicates  the

55
00:01:41,294 --> 00:01:42,934
important  points  well.

56
00:01:42,934 --> 00:01:44,733
The  right  question  is  not  which

57
00:01:44,733 --> 00:01:47,133
format  looks most  impressive,  but

58
00:01:47,133 --> 00:01:48,773
which  format  makes  this  message

59
00:01:48,773 --> 00:01:51,014
clearer?  Clarity  should  guide  the

60
00:01:51,014 --> 00:01:52,415
choice  every  time.

61
00:01:52,655 --> 00:01:54,895
Storytelling  can  be  useful  because

62
00:01:54,895 --> 00:01:56,815
it  gives  information  a  natural

63
00:01:56,815 --> 00:01:58,655
order.  You  might  begin  with  the

64
00:01:58,655 --> 00:02:00,735
moment  the  problem  became  visible,

65
00:02:00,735 --> 00:02:02,295
then  explain  the  decision  that  was

66
00:02:02,295 --> 00:02:03,775
made,  what  happened  during  the

67
00:02:03,775 --> 00:02:05,655
response,  and  what  changed

68
00:02:05,655 --> 00:02:06,974
afterward.

69
00:02:06,974 --> 00:02:08,534
Visuals  can  support  the  story,

70
00:02:08,534 --> 00:02:10,335
too.  A  before  and  after

71
00:02:10,335 --> 00:02:13,175
comparison,  a  short  timeline,  one

72
00:02:13,175 --> 00:02:15,614
meaningful  photo,  or  a  few  numbers

73
00:02:15,614 --> 00:02:17,014
may  communicate  more  than  a

74
00:02:17,014 --> 00:02:19,735
crowded  slide.  Use  visuals  to  make

75
00:02:19,735 --> 00:02:22,055
the  message  easier  to  understand.

76
00:02:22,055 --> 00:02:23,934
Not to  decorate  it.

77
00:02:23,934 --> 00:02:26,015
If  a  visual  adds  noise,  it  is

78
00:02:26,015 --> 00:02:28,535
probably  unnecessary.  The  message

79
00:02:28,535 --> 00:02:30,015
should  always  remain  the  main

80
00:02:30,015 --> 00:02:30,873
focus.

81
00:02:30,873 --> 00:02:33,512
When  speaking,  clarity  matters  more

82
00:02:33,512 --> 00:02:34,913
than  performance.

83
00:02:34,913 --> 00:02:37,073
Use  a  natural  posture,  clear

84
00:02:37,073 --> 00:02:39,153
gestures  and  facial  expressions

85
00:02:39,153 --> 00:02:41,073
that  fit  what  you  are  saying.

86
00:02:41,073 --> 00:02:42,592
Vary  your  tone  enough  that  the

87
00:02:42,592 --> 00:02:44,833
explanation  does  not  become  flat

88
00:02:44,833 --> 00:02:46,592
and  try  to  reduce  distracting

89
00:02:46,592 --> 00:02:48,193
filler  words.

90
00:02:48,193 --> 00:02:50,033
A  strong  opening  helps  people

91
00:02:50,033 --> 00:02:52,232
understand  why  they  should  listen

92
00:02:52,232 --> 00:02:53,753
and  a  strong  ending  helps  them

93
00:02:53,753 --> 00:02:55,953
remember  what  mattered  most.

94
00:02:55,953 --> 00:02:58,392
In  between,  repeat  only  the  ideas

95
00:02:58,392 --> 00:03:00,912
that  genuinely  deserve  emphasis.

96
00:03:00,912 --> 00:03:02,193
The  goal  is  not  constant

97
00:03:02,193 --> 00:03:04,272
entertainment.  It  is  clear,

98
00:03:04,272 --> 00:03:06,353
memorable  communication.

99
00:03:06,353 --> 00:03:08,393
Simple  delivery  can  still  be  very

100
00:03:08,393 --> 00:03:09,289
effective.

101
00:03:09,289 --> 00:03:12,609
Try  a  short  individual  exercise.

102
00:03:12,609 --> 00:03:14,449
Imagine  you  have  only  five

103
00:03:14,449 --> 00:03:16,449
sentences  to  explain  a  small

104
00:03:16,449 --> 00:03:17,729
initiative.

105
00:03:17,729 --> 00:03:19,289
Sentence  one,  what  was  the

106
00:03:19,289 --> 00:03:22,209
problem?  Sentence  two,  what  need

107
00:03:22,209 --> 00:03:24,729
did  you  identify?  Sentence  three,

108
00:03:24,729 --> 00:03:27,049
what  response  was  used?  Sentence

109
00:03:27,049 --> 00:03:30,249
four,  what  happened?  Sentence  five,

110
00:03:30,249 --> 00:03:31,689
what  was  learned?

111
00:03:31,689 --> 00:03:33,849
If  those  five  sentences  are  clear,

112
00:03:33,849 --> 00:03:35,689
you  already  have  the  core  of  a

113
00:03:35,689 --> 00:03:38,209
strong  explanation.  Everything  else

114
00:03:38,209 --> 00:03:40,249
should  support  that

115
00:03:40,249 --> 00:03:41,848
This  is  a  useful  test  because  it

116
00:03:41,848 --> 00:03:43,609
forces  you  to  separate  the  most

117
00:03:43,609 --> 00:03:45,368
important  information  from  the

118
00:03:45,368 --> 00:03:47,289
details  that  can  be  left  out.  It

119
00:03:47,289 --> 00:03:49,049
also  shows  whether  the  main  idea

120
00:03:49,049 --> 00:03:50,414
is  truly  clear.

121
00:03:50,414 --> 00:03:52,694
Before  we  close,  let's  look  back

122
00:03:52,694 --> 00:03:54,894
at  the  whole  journey  of  act.  It

123
00:03:54,894 --> 00:03:56,894
started  with  you.  In  the  first

124
00:03:56,894 --> 00:03:59,454
unit,  the  focus  was  self-awareness,

125
00:03:59,454 --> 00:04:01,214
understanding  your  identity,  your

126
00:04:01,214 --> 00:04:03,053
strengths,  your  habits,  and  the

127
00:04:03,053 --> 00:04:04,694
way  you  naturally  respond  to

128
00:04:04,694 --> 00:04:06,854
different  situations.

129
00:04:06,854 --> 00:04:09,174
Then,  the  focus  moved  outward.  You

130
00:04:09,174 --> 00:04:10,773
looked  at  the  people  around  you,

131
00:04:10,773 --> 00:04:12,054
the  roles  you  have,  the

132
00:04:12,054 --> 00:04:13,773
responsibilities  connected  to  those

133
00:04:13,773 --> 00:04:15,494
roles,  and  the  communities  you  are

134
00:04:15,494 --> 00:04:16,693
part  of.

135
00:04:16,693 --> 00:04:17,814
The  goal  was  to  see  that

136
00:04:17,814 --> 00:04:19,693
understanding  yourself  is  only  the

137
00:04:19,693 --> 00:04:21,853
beginning.  Awareness  becomes  more

138
00:04:21,853 --> 00:04:23,054
useful  when  it  helps  you

139
00:04:23,054 --> 00:04:24,614
understand  other  people  and  the

140
00:04:24,614 --> 00:04:26,164
environment  around  you.

141
00:04:26,164 --> 00:04:28,604
From  there,  act  moved  into  social

142
00:04:28,604 --> 00:04:30,564
issues.  You  learn  to  choose  a

143
00:04:30,564 --> 00:04:33,003
real  problem.  Define  it  clearly,

144
00:04:33,003 --> 00:04:35,084
identify  who  is  affected  and  look

145
00:04:35,084 --> 00:04:37,044
for  useful  information  instead  of

146
00:04:37,044 --> 00:04:39,084
jumping  straight  to  a  solution.

147
00:04:39,084 --> 00:04:40,883
Then  you  separated  the  problem

148
00:04:40,883 --> 00:04:43,084
from  the  actual  need,  generated

149
00:04:43,084 --> 00:04:44,884
different  ideas,  compared  those

150
00:04:44,884 --> 00:04:46,924
ideas  and  chose  the  one  that  fit

151
00:04:46,924 --> 00:04:48,084
best.

152
00:04:48,084 --> 00:04:49,283
You  also  learned  how  to  make  a

153
00:04:49,283 --> 00:04:51,324
solution  smart,  so  it  becomes

154
00:04:51,324 --> 00:04:53,924
specific,  measurable,  attainable,

155
00:04:53,924 --> 00:04:55,884
relevant,  and  timely.

156
00:04:55,884 --> 00:04:58,004
The  important  lesson  was  simple.

157
00:04:58,004 --> 00:05:00,004
Better  solutions  usually  begin  with

158
00:05:00,004 --> 00:05:02,164
better  understanding,  not  faster

159
00:05:02,164 --> 00:05:02,997
action.

160
00:05:02,997 --> 00:05:04,956
In  the  final  unit,  the  question

161
00:05:04,956 --> 00:05:07,197
became  how  could  a  good  solution

162
00:05:07,197 --> 00:05:09,437
become  practical?  You  learn  to

163
00:05:09,437 --> 00:05:11,197
break  a  project  into  manageable

164
00:05:11,197 --> 00:05:14,037
tasks,  think  about  resources,  cost,

165
00:05:14,037 --> 00:05:16,676
responsibility,  time  and  place,

166
00:05:16,676 --> 00:05:18,596
then  review  the  plan  instead  of

167
00:05:18,596 --> 00:05:19,957
assuming  the  first  version  is

168
00:05:19,957 --> 00:05:21,157
perfect.

169
00:05:21,157 --> 00:05:23,077
You  also  saw  how  experience  can

170
00:05:23,077 --> 00:05:25,317
improve  planning,  how  execution  may

171
00:05:25,317 --> 00:05:27,196
require  adaptation,  why

172
00:05:27,196 --> 00:05:29,196
documentation  matters,  and  how

173
00:05:29,196 --> 00:05:31,196
reflection  turns  activity  into

174
00:05:31,196 --> 00:05:32,436
learning.

175
00:05:32,436 --> 00:05:33,877
Finally,  You  learned  how  to

176
00:05:33,877 --> 00:05:36,517
explain  the  experience  clearly.

177
00:05:36,517 --> 00:05:38,637
Put  together,  the  full  path  is

178
00:05:38,637 --> 00:05:41,076
understand,  identify  the  need,

179
00:05:41,076 --> 00:05:45,757
choose,  plan,  act,  adapt,  document,

180
00:05:45,757 --> 00:05:46,622
and  learn.

181
00:05:46,622 --> 00:05:49,182
And  that  brings  us  to  the  end  of

182
00:05:49,182 --> 00:05:50,341
act.

183
00:05:50,341 --> 00:05:52,622
You  do  not  need  to  remember  every

184
00:05:52,622 --> 00:05:55,662
example,  every  diagram  or  every

185
00:05:55,662 --> 00:05:57,742
phrase  from  the  course.

186
00:05:57,742 --> 00:05:59,662
Keep  the  way  of  thinking  with

187
00:05:59,662 --> 00:06:00,662
you.

188
00:06:00,662 --> 00:06:03,661
Understand  before  you  decide.

189
00:06:03,661 --> 00:06:06,262
Listen  before  you  assume.

190
00:06:06,262 --> 00:06:07,942
Choose  carefully,

191
00:06:07,942 --> 00:06:09,862
plan  clearly,

192
00:06:09,862 --> 00:06:12,782
stay  flexible  when  reality  changes

193
00:06:12,782 --> 00:06:15,661
and  learn  from  what  happens.

194
00:06:15,661 --> 00:06:17,061
Thank  you  for  spend ending  this

195
00:06:17,061 --> 00:06:18,662
journey  with  us.

196
00:06:18,662 --> 00:06:20,181
From  all  of  us  at  Nama

197
00:06:20,181 --> 00:06:22,822
Foundation,  we  hope  act  leaves  you

198
00:06:22,822 --> 00:06:24,662
with  ideas  you  can  use  in  your

199
00:06:24,662 --> 00:06:27,822
studies,  your  work,  your  community,

200
00:06:27,822 --> 00:06:29,462
and  the  choices  you  make  every

201
00:06:29,462 --> 00:06:30,822
day.

202
00:06:30,822 --> 00:06:33,542
Take  care,  keep  learning,  and  we

203
00:06:33,542 --> 00:06:35,038
hope  to  see  you  again.

